{
    "title": "The Commerce Control List",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 05, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-05/pdf/2025-22137.pdf",
    "summary": "Today\u2019s Federal Register bundles three practical updates: a fix to the Commerce Department\u2019s export rules for certain metal materials, a temporary boating safety zone in Charleston Harbor for the Parade of Boats on December 13, and federal approval of Texas and Oklahoma plans to cut haze and improve visibility.\n\nThe export rules correction restores language that clarifies what counts as \u201cmetals\u201d and \u201calloys,\u201d including crude and semi\u2011finished forms. This helps exporters, freight forwarders, and compliance teams know exactly what falls under control, reducing confusion and the risk of missteps. Companies that hoped to ship borderline \u201cfinished\u201d items that are really basic forms may find tighter interpretations and could face added paperwork or delays.\n\nFor Charleston, the Coast Guard will enforce a safety zone from 5 p.m. to 9 p.m. on December 13 during the Parade of Boats. Spectators, local communities, and event organizers benefit from clearer navigation rules and a safer waterfront. Recreational boaters and some commercial operators may be inconvenienced by temporary no\u2011go areas and the need to follow directions from patrol vessels, but the limits are short and announced in advance.\n\nFinally, the Environmental Protection Agency approved parts of Texas and Oklahoma plans to reduce haze, including earlier milestones and progress updates. Parks, communities, and visitors stand to gain from clearer skies and healthier air, while state agencies and industry get more certainty about what\u2019s expected next. Some power plants and industrial facilities may face added costs to keep meeting goals, and there could be operational changes as measures are implemented, but approval also means fewer regulatory gaps and clearer timelines.\n\nOverall, these actions aim to clarify rules, improve safety, and advance cleaner air. The benefits are clearer instructions for exporters, safer waters for a popular local event, and steadier progress on visibility and health. The trade\u2011offs include tighter compliance for some shippers, short\u2011term limits for boaters, and potential costs for certain facilities as states carry out their air plans.",
    "embedding": [
